-- consolidation-worksheet-elimination-entries
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>Consolidated statements are never recorded in anyone's ledger. They are built on a worksheet that adds the parent's and the subsidiaries' statements line by line and then removes what is internal to the group: the parent's investment against the subsidiary's equity, intragroup balances, and intragroup income, expenses and dividends (PSAK 110 B86).</p></li><li><p>The elimination entries run in a fixed order. Reverse the parent's income from the subsidiary and the subsidiary's dividends, record the non-controlling interest's (NCI's) share, eliminate opening equity against the investment and opening NCI, allocate and depreciate the excess paid over book value, and cancel reciprocal balances. The worksheet has three parts, profit or loss, then retained earnings, then the balance sheet, each feeding the next.</p></li><li><p>The method the parent uses in its own books (cost or equity method, both allowed by PSAK 227) changes the first entry and adds one conversion entry, but never the consolidated result. A worksheet that balances can still be wrong, so the ties to the parent's equity and to NCI matter more than the totals.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Barito Pacific Tbk and subsidiaries (BRPT) list each subsidiary's "total assets before elimination" in their 2025 statements. PT Barito Renewables Energy Tbk (BREN), 64.67 percent owned, has 3,867,410 at 31 December 2025, in USD thousand (BRPT FS 2025, Note 1d, PDF p. 26). What happens between such "before elimination" figures and the group's statements is the subject of this page.</p><p>After reading it a student should be able to prepare a three-part consolidation worksheet in the year of acquisition and in later years, write each elimination entry in order, adapt the worksheet to a parent that uses the cost method, and find the error when the worksheet does not balance.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from BRPT's and BREN's statements are cited to their note; every other number is an illustration. Control, NCI measurement and profit attribution are taught in the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest">Non-Controlling Interest essay</a> and used here without re-deriving them.</p><h2>1. The parent's own books decide what must be eliminated</h2><p>The parent's investment in a subsidiary sits in its own books as one asset. How that asset is measured in the parent's <em>separate financial statements</em> (the parent's own statements, in which subsidiaries are investments) is a policy choice. PSAK 227 <em>Separate Financial Statements</em> (formerly PSAK 4, IAS 27) allows cost, PSAK 109 or the equity method, applied to each category of investment consistently (para. 10). The equity-method option was added in Indonesia by the amendment to PSAK 4 ratified in November 2015 and effective from 1 January 2016, following the 2014 IAS 27 amendment. Dividends from a subsidiary go to profit or loss under cost or PSAK 109, and reduce the investment under the equity method (para. 12).</p><p>This page assumes the parent uses the complete equity method. The parent books its share of the subsidiary's profit after the depreciation of the excess it paid over book value, so its profit equals the consolidated profit attributable to its owners. Beams calls this <em>one-line consolidation</em>; the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments">Long-Term Investments essay</a> (section 5) explains the mechanism.</p><p><strong>The running illustration.</strong> BRPT holds 64.67 percent of BREN (Note 1d). Its interest in BREN's geothermal business came through common-control transactions. BRPT acquired Star Energy Group Holdings (SEGHPL) in 2018, pooled as if combined from the start (Note 1d, PDF p. 52) at book values (policy Note 3e, PDF p. 70), and BREN acquired SEGHPL from BRPT in 2022 for new BREN shares (BREN FS 2025, Note 19b, PDF p. 115). No fair value excess arose at BRPT's level, although goodwill from Star Energy's earlier purchase of Salak and Darajat was carried across at its carrying amount; the Business Combinations essay (K1) covers both. To show every entry, suppose instead (illustration) that on 1 January 2024 BRPT had bought 65 percent of a geothermal company from outside owners. The subsidiary is called Sub. A <em>fair value uplift</em> is the amount by which an asset's fair value at acquisition exceeds its carrying amount in the subsidiary's books.</p><blockquote><p>Consideration paid = 520,000</p><p>Sub's book equity = share capital 400,000 + retained earnings 200,000 = 600,000</p><p>Fair value uplifts: power plant +100,000 (20 years left, so 5,000 a year); spare-parts inventory +10,000 (used in 2024)</p><p>Identifiable net assets at fair value = 600,000 + 100,000 + 10,000 = 710,000</p><p>NCI at its proportionate share = 35% × 710,000 = 248,500</p><p>Goodwill = 520,000 + 248,500 − 710,000 = 58,500</p><p>Excess over book value = 520,000 + 248,500 − 600,000 = 168,500 = 100,000 + 10,000 + 58,500</p></blockquote><p>PSAK 103 lets the acquirer choose fair value or the proportionate share for NCI, deal by deal (essay 25, section 2). The proportionate share is used here. Deferred tax on the uplifts is ignored to keep the steps short; K1 adds it.</p><p>Sub earns 90,000 and pays dividends of 40,000 in 2024, then earns 110,000 and declares dividends of 50,000 in 2025, of which 20,000 is still unpaid at year end.</p><blockquote><p>2024 profit as the group sees it = 90,000 − 10,000 inventory − 5,000 plant = 75,000; parent 65% = 48,750; NCI 35% = 26,250</p><p>2025 profit as the group sees it = 110,000 − 5,000 plant = 105,000; parent 65% = 68,250; NCI 35% = 36,750</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Parent's books</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cost method</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Equity method</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1 Jan 2024, purchase</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Investment in Sub 520,000 / Cr Cash 520,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>same</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024 profit of Sub</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no entry</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Investment in Sub 48,750 / Cr Income from Sub 48,750</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024 dividend, 65% × 40,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 26,000 / Cr Dividend income 26,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 26,000 / Cr Investment in Sub 26,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2025 profit of Sub</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no entry</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Investment in Sub 68,250 / Cr Income from Sub 68,250</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2025 dividend, 65% × 50,000 = 32,500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 19,500, Dr Dividend receivable 13,000 / Cr Dividend income 32,500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 19,500, Dr Dividend receivable 13,000 / Cr Investment in Sub 32,500</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Investment at 31 Dec 2025</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>520,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>520,000 + 48,750 − 26,000 + 68,250 − 32,500 = 578,500</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The equity-method balance already contains the parent's share of Sub's retained profits and of the depreciated uplift. The cost-method balance contains neither, so the worksheet has to put them there before it can eliminate anything (section 6).</p><h2>2. The worksheet has three parts, and each feeds the next</h2><p>Each part of the worksheet ends in the figure the next part needs. The worksheet stacks three parts (income statement, retained earnings statement, balance sheet), with columns for the parent, the subsidiary, the eliminations and the consolidated result.</p><ul><li><p>Profit attributable to the owners of the parent, the last line of part one, is carried into part two.</p></li><li><p>Closing retained earnings, the last line of part two, is carried into part three.</p></li><li><p>Part three must then balance, total assets against total liabilities and equity.</p></li></ul><p>PSAK 201 replaced the retained earnings statement with a statement of changes in equity, but the logic is unchanged. Every year starts again from the separate statements and repeats the entries for past events, such as the acquisition-date allocation.</p><p>PSAK 110 <em>Consolidated Financial Statements</em> (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10) describes the three steps the entries perform (B86): combine like items of assets, liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows; offset the parent's investment against its portion of the subsidiary's equity; and eliminate intragroup assets, liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows in full. BRPT's policy repeats the third step: "All intragroup assets and liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows relating to transactions between members of the Group are eliminated in full on consolidation" (Note 3c, PDF p. 68).</p><h2>3. The standard elimination sequence</h2><p>There are six entries, made in a fixed order. Each removes something that would otherwise be counted twice.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Step</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Problem it solves</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Entry (equity-method parent)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(1) Income from subsidiary and dividends</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>The parent's income line repeats Sub's revenues and expenses; dividends to the parent move cash inside the group</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Income from Sub / Cr Dividends declared (Sub, parent's share); Cr Investment for the difference</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2) NCI share</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Consolidated profit belongs partly to outside owners, and their dividends reduce their claim</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr NCI share of profit / Cr Dividends declared (NCI's share); Cr NCI for the difference</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(3) Opening equity against investment and NCI</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Sub's opening equity is already represented by the investment and opening NCI</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Share capital and opening retained earnings (Sub), Dr Unamortised excess / Cr Investment (opening), Cr NCI (opening)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(4) Allocate the excess</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>The <em>unamortised excess</em> is a worksheet suspense account to be assigned to the assets it paid for</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Plant and equipment, Dr Goodwill (and inventory or cost of sales) / Cr Unamortised excess</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(5) Depreciate the excess</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Group depreciation follows acquisition-date fair values (PSAK 110 B88); Sub depreciates book values</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Depreciation / Cr Plant and equipment</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(6) Reciprocal balances</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Balances between group companies are neither assets nor liabilities of the group</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr the payable / Cr the receivable; Dr intragroup income / Cr intragroup expense</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Entries (1) and (2) together remove all of Sub's dividends. Entry (1) returns the investment to its opening balance, which is why entry (3) can eliminate it against opening equity. The intragroup profit eliminations of K3 and K4 can sit anywhere in the sequence without changing the result.</p><h2>4. The year of acquisition</h2><p>In the first year the opening equity in entry (3) is the equity at the acquisition date, and the inventory uplift reaches cost of sales because the spares were used during the year. For example (illustration), the 2024 worksheet for Sub:</p><blockquote><p>(1) Dr Income from Sub 48,750 / Cr Dividends declared 26,000; Cr Investment in Sub 22,750</p><p>(2) Dr NCI share of profit 26,250 / Cr Dividends declared 14,000; Cr NCI 12,250</p><p>(3) Dr Share capital, Sub 400,000; Dr Retained earnings, Sub 200,000; Dr Unamortised excess 168,500 / Cr Investment in Sub 520,000; Cr NCI 248,500</p><p>(4) Dr Cost of sales 10,000; Dr Plant and equipment 100,000; Dr Goodwill 58,500 / Cr Unamortised excess 168,500</p><p>(5) Dr Depreciation 5,000 / Cr Plant and equipment 5,000</p></blockquote><p>Entry (3) balances at 768,500 on each side. The investment, 520,000 + 48,750 − 26,000 = 542,750, is credited 22,750 + 520,000 = 542,750 and falls to zero. NCI closes at 248,500 + 12,250 = 260,750, which is 35 percent of Sub's net assets at group carrying amounts (book values plus the uplifts not yet written off).</p><blockquote><p>35% × (600,000 + 90,000 − 40,000 + 100,000 − 5,000) = 35% × 745,000 = 260,750</p></blockquote><p>Under the proportionate method goodwill is not part of NCI. Had control been obtained during the year, Sub's income and expenses would enter only from the acquisition date (B88), a case K5 takes up.</p><h2>5. A later year: the full worksheet</h2><p>From the second year on, entry (3) uses Sub's retained earnings at the start of the year and the excess still unamortised at that date. In 2025 three intragroup transactions also need entry (6).</p><ul><li><p>On 1 July 2025 the parent lent Sub 30,000 at 6 percent a year. Half a year's interest, 30,000 × 6% × 6/12 = 900, is accrued by both and unpaid.</p></li><li><p>On 30 December Sub repaid 5,000 of the loan by bank transfer, which reached the parent on 2 January. Sub shows a loan payable of 25,000, while the parent still shows a receivable of 30,000. The 5,000 is <em>cash in transit</em>, money that has left one group company and not yet reached the other.</p></li><li><p>Sub's unpaid dividend of 20,000 includes 13,000 owed to the parent.</p></li></ul><p>For example (illustration), the opening amounts for 2025:</p><blockquote><p>Sub retained earnings, 1 Jan 2025 = 200,000 + 90,000 − 40,000 = 250,000</p><p>Unamortised excess, 1 Jan 2025 = 168,500 − 10,000 − 5,000 = 153,500 (plant 95,000 + goodwill 58,500)</p><p>Investment, 1 Jan 2025 = 542,750; NCI, 1 Jan 2025 = 260,750</p></blockquote><p>The entries:</p><blockquote><p>(1) Dr Income from Sub 68,250 / Cr Dividends declared 32,500; Cr Investment in Sub 35,750</p><p>(2) Dr NCI share of profit 36,750 / Cr Dividends declared 17,500; Cr NCI 19,250</p><p>(3) Dr Share capital, Sub 400,000; Dr Retained earnings 1 Jan, Sub 250,000; Dr Unamortised excess 153,500 / Cr Investment in Sub 542,750; Cr NCI 260,750</p><p>(4) Dr Plant and equipment 95,000; Dr Goodwill 58,500 / Cr Unamortised excess 153,500</p><p>(5) Dr Depreciation 5,000 / Cr Plant and equipment 5,000</p><p>(6a) Dr Cash in transit 5,000 / Cr Loan to Sub 5,000</p><p>(6b) Dr Loan from parent 25,000 / Cr Loan to Sub 25,000</p><p>(6c) Dr Interest payable 900 / Cr Interest receivable 900</p><p>(6d) Dr Interest income 900 / Cr Finance costs 900</p><p>(6e) Dr Dividend payable 13,000 / Cr Dividend receivable 13,000</p></blockquote><p>Entry (6a) is an adjustment, not an elimination; the reciprocal balances must agree before (6b) cancels them. A cheque in transit is handled the same way, with an adjustment such as "Cash 20,000 / Note receivable – Sub 20,000" ahead of the eliminations.</p><p><strong>The worksheet, Parent and Sub, year ended 31 December 2025 (illustration).</strong> Credit balances in the income statement and equity are shown positive, expenses and dividends in parentheses; the elimination columns show the entry number.</p><table style="min-width: 150px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Parent</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Sub</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cr</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Consolidated</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Income statement</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Revenue</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>200,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>355,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>555,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest income</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>900</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(6d) 900</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Income from Sub</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>68,250</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(1) 68,250</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Operating expenses</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(120,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(150,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(270,000)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Depreciation</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(40,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(5) 5,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(45,000)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Finance costs</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(20,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(30,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(6d) 900</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(49,100)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Income tax</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(15,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(25,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(40,000)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Profit for the year</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>114,150</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>110,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>150,900</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NCI share of profit</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2) 36,750</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(36,750)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Profit attributable to owners of the parent</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>114,150</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>110,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>114,150</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Retained earnings</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Retained earnings, 1 January</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>400,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>250,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(3) 250,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>400,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Profit attributable to owners of the parent</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>114,150</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>110,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>114,150</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dividends declared</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(60,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(50,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(1) 32,500; (2) 17,500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(60,000)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Retained earnings, 31 December</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>454,150</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>310,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>454,150</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Balance sheet</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>50,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>60,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>110,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash in transit</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(6a) 5,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>5,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Receivables</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>40,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>70,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>110,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dividend receivable</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>13,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(6e) 13,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest receivable</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>900</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(6c) 900</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Loan to Sub</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>30,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(6a) 5,000; (6b) 25,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Inventories</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>30,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>30,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Investment in Sub</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>578,500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(1) 35,750; (3) 542,750</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Plant and equipment, net</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>500,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>900,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(4) 95,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(5) 5,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,490,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Goodwill</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(4) 58,500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>58,500</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Unamortised excess</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(3) 153,500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(4) 153,500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total assets</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,212,400</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,060,000</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,803,500</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Trade payables</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>58,250</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>45,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>103,250</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dividend payable</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>20,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(6e) 13,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>7,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest payable</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>900</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(6c) 900</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Loan from parent</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>25,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(6b) 25,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Bonds and bank loans</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>200,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>259,100</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>459,100</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Share capital</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>500,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>400,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(3) 400,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>500,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Retained earnings, from above</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>454,150</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>310,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>454,150</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NCI, 1 January</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(3) 260,750</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NCI, increase for the year</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2) 19,250</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>280,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total liabilities and equity</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,212,400</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,060,000</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,111,800</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,111,800</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>1,803,500</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The 1,111,800 of debits equals the 1,111,800 of credits, and total assets of 1,803,500 equal liabilities of 569,350 plus share capital 500,000, retained earnings 454,150 and NCI 280,000.</p><blockquote><p>Consolidated profit attributable to owners = parent's equity-method profit = 114,150</p><p>Consolidated retained earnings = parent's equity-method retained earnings = 454,150</p><p>NCI = 35% × (Sub net assets 710,000 + plant uplift remaining 90,000) = 35% × 800,000 = 280,000</p></blockquote><p>The 7,000 of dividend payable left after (6e) is owed to Sub's outside shareholders, a real group liability.</p><h2>6. A parent on the cost method</h2><p>A parent using the cost method reaches the same consolidated statements by changing two entries. Its investment still stands at 520,000, its opening retained earnings lack its share of Sub's retained profits, and its profit carries dividend income instead of the share of profit.</p><blockquote><p>Parent's 2025 profit, cost method = 114,150 − 68,250 + 32,500 = 78,400</p><p>Conversion to equity basis at 1 Jan 2025 = 48,750 − 26,000 = 22,750</p><p>Parent's retained earnings, 1 Jan 2025, cost method = 400,000 − 22,750 = 377,250</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Worksheet entry</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cost-method parent</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Equity-method parent</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(0) Convert opening balances</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Investment in Sub 22,750 / Cr Retained earnings 1 Jan, parent 22,750</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>not needed</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(1)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Dividend income 32,500 / Cr Dividends declared 32,500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Income from Sub 68,250 / Cr Dividends declared 32,500; Cr Investment in Sub 35,750</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2) to (6)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>identical</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>identical</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Entry (0) lifts the investment to 520,000 + 22,750 = 542,750, the opening balance entry (3) eliminates, and lifts the parent's opening retained earnings to the equity-method 400,000. The 22,750 is the parent's share of Sub's retained profits since acquisition, 65% × (250,000 − 200,000) = 32,500, less its share of the excess already written off, 65% × 15,000 = 9,750. Entry (1) then removes only the dividend income, because the 68,250 share of profit was never in the parent's column, and the consolidated column comes out as in section 5. This section follows Beams and PSAK 227.</p><h2>7. Uniform policies and reporting dates</h2><p>The worksheet adds like items only if they were measured alike and at the same date. PSAK 110 requires adjustments when a group member uses accounting policies other than the group's (B87). BRPT's policy says adjustments are made "when necessary ... to bring their accounting policies in line with the Group's accounting policies" (Note 3c, PDF p. 67). For example (illustration, separate from the section 5 worksheet), suppose Sub carries an office building it rents out at fair value under PSAK 240 (formerly PSAK 13, IAS 40), while the group uses the cost model. Sub bought the building on 1 January 2025 and booked a fair value gain of 3,000 at year end; the group's cost-model depreciation would be 400. Before the eliminations:</p><blockquote><p>Dr Fair value gain 3,000; Dr Depreciation 400 / Cr Investment property 3,400</p></blockquote><p>Like any adjustment to Sub's figures, its effect on profit is shared with NCI, so NCI's share of profit falls by 35% × 3,400 = 1,190.</p><p>Reporting dates should be the same. If that is impracticable, the subsidiary's most recent statements may be used, adjusted for significant transactions or events in between, provided the gap is no more than three months and the length of periods and the gap stay the same from period to period (B92–B93). PSAK 110 requires such events to be adjusted for, not merely disclosed. For example (illustration), if Sub's year ended on 30 September and the parent lent it 10,000 on 15 November, Sub's statements would show no loan while the parent's showed a receivable. The adjustment comes first, then the elimination.</p><blockquote><p>Adjust Sub: Dr Cash 10,000 / Cr Loan from parent 10,000</p><p>Eliminate: Dr Loan from parent 10,000 / Cr Loan to Sub 10,000</p></blockquote><p>A subsidiary with another functional currency is first translated under PSAK 221 (formerly PSAK 10, IAS 21; see <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/other-comprehensive-income">other-comprehensive-income</a>).</p><h2>8. Finding the error when the worksheet does not balance</h2><p>Most errors show up in the balance sheet part, where totals stop agreeing; checking each account one by one works, but testing the entries first is faster.</p><ol><li><p><strong>Each entry balances on its own.</strong> For example (illustration), an equity elimination that reads "Retained earnings 50,000; Capital stock – S 250,000; Unamortized excess 150,000 / Investment in S 360,000; Noncontrolling interest 40,000" has debits of 450,000 and credits of 400,000. If S's capital stock is 200,000, the entry posted as written would leave the worksheet out by 50,000.</p></li><li><p><strong>The investment and the subsidiary's equity accounts fall to zero.</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Each reciprocal pair cancels.</strong> Leave out entry (6a) in section 5 and the worksheet still balances. A loan to Sub of 5,000 survives in the consolidated column and cash is 5,000 short, an asset the group does not have in place of one it does.</p></li><li><p><strong>The ties hold.</strong> Consolidated profit and retained earnings attributable to owners equal the parent's under the equity method, and NCI equals its share of the subsidiary's net assets at group carrying amounts.</p></li></ol><p>Check 4 catches errors that balance. For example (illustration), a balance-sheet elimination one year after acquisition that reads "Capital stock 60,000; Retained earnings 20,000; Goodwill 40,000 / Investment in S 108,000; Noncontrolling interest 12,000" balances at 120,000, but if S's year-end retained earnings are 40,000 and goodwill is 20,000, goodwill is overstated by 20,000 and 20,000 of S's retained earnings survive into the consolidated column.</p><h2>9. Consolidation procedures across industries</h2><p><strong>Groups in layers.</strong> A listed holding group can consolidate sub-group by sub-group. BREN's own statements already consolidate the Star Energy companies, so BREN's NCI can enter BRPT's worksheet as part of BREN's equity. Note 1d shows the chain, with PT Barito Wind Energy held 99.99 percent "through BREN" and PT Nefin Solar Asset 51 percent through Barito Wind Energy (PDF p. 26). K6 works through the effective interests.</p><p><strong>Banks and structured entities.</strong> Banks consolidate securitisation vehicles and other <em>structured entities</em>, designed so that voting rights do not decide control, when the PSAK 110 control test is met; BRPT's policy also covers them (Note 3c, PDF p. 67).</p><p><strong>Investment entities.</strong> An investment entity does not consolidate its investees, except subsidiaries that provide services for its investment activities, and measures them at fair value through profit or loss (PSAK 110 paras. 31–32).</p><p><strong>Mining and oil and gas.</strong> A participating interest held under a joint operating agreement is usually a joint operation under PSAK 111 (formerly PSAK 66, IFRS 11), and without joint control is still recognised line by line (para. 23), so there is no investment to eliminate and no NCI.</p><p><strong>Islamic finance.</strong> A sharia subsidiary of a conventional bank is consolidated with the same entries, but it reports under PSAK 401 (formerly PSAK 101) with accounts such as murabahah receivables and temporary <em>syirkah</em> funds, which must be mapped to the group's chart of accounts first (STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED).</p><h2>10. Presentation and analysis: Barito Pacific's Notes 1d, 34 and 46</h2><p>Three BRPT notes show the worksheet's outputs. Note 34 splits NCI by sub-group and gives BREN's summarised figures "before intragroup eliminations" (PDF pp. 220–222). BREN's own consolidated statements give the equity those figures come from (BREN FS 2025, statement of financial position, PDF p. 12).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2025</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>BREN equity attributable to its owners (BREN FS)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>636,868</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>516,862</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Outside share of BREN's outstanding shares (net of treasury)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>35.33114%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>35.3340%</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Outside share × equity attributable</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>225,013</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>182,628</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Add BREN's own NCI (BREN FS)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>246,651</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>215,671</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Expected NCI for "BREN and its subsidiaries"</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>471,664</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>398,299</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Reported in BRPT Note 34</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>471,951</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>397,199</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Difference</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>287</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(1,100)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Outside share 2025 = 1 − 86,514,146,666 / (133,786,220,000 − 6,000,000 treasury) = 35.33114%; 2024, with no treasury shares = 1 − 86,514,146,666 / 133,786,220,000 = 35.3340% (BREN FS 2025, Note 19a, PDF p. 114)</p></blockquote><p>With no fair value excess at BRPT's level, NCI should be the outside share of BREN's book equity plus BREN's own NCI, which is what entry (3) and the NCI roll-forward produce. The reported figure is within about 0.1 percent of that prediction in 2025 and 0.3 percent in 2024. The residuals are unexplained; BRPT does not reconcile them.</p><p>The segment note shows elimination at work on income (Note 46, PDF p. 239). Inter-segment revenue of 4,538 in 2025 (property 4,505 and others 33) is removed to reach consolidated revenue of 7,630,662, against 5,136 in 2024. The energy and resources segment's assets of 3,867,410 equal BREN's total assets, so the segment is BREN's consolidated statements. The eliminations column also adds 139,079 to assets and 9,339 to depreciation in 2025 (150,683 and 8,897 in 2024). Adjustments booked in no segment's ledger and depreciated each year behave like entries (4) and (5), but BRPT does not say what they are, and with no excess on BREN they may relate to another acquisition or to other group-level adjustments.</p><h2>11. Related essays</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest">non-controlling-interest</a>: control, NCI measurement, attribution, ownership changes.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments">long-term-investments</a>: the equity method in an investor's books.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/intangible-assets-net">intangible-assets-net</a>: goodwill after acquisition and its impairment.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/other-comprehensive-income">other-comprehensive-income</a>: translation of foreign subsidiaries before consolidation.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/retained-earnings">retained-earnings</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/share-capital">other-equity-component</a>: what consolidated retained earnings and NCI-transaction reserves contain.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/consolidation/business-combinations-acquisition-method-goodwill-common-control">K1</a>: the acquisition method, the allocation of excess, deferred tax on uplifts, and common control.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/consolidation/intercompany-profit-in-inventory">K3</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/consolidation/intercompany-profit-plant-assets">K4</a>: the intragroup profit entries.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/consolidation/consolidated-cash-flows-and-mid-year-acquisitions">K5</a>: the consolidated cash flow statement and mid-year acquisitions.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/consolidation/mutual-holdings-complex-group-structures">K6</a>: layered and mutual holdings.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>The NCI residuals in section 10 (287 in 2025, (1,100) in 2024) are unexplained. They are too large for rounding of the ownership percentage, and BREN's 2025 treasury shares (6,000,000, 0.01 percent) are already in the computation. Separately, Note 34 gives BREN's 2024 total assets as 3,798,375, liabilities as 3,062,313 and profit as 158,612, while BREN's own audited 2024 figures, unchanged as comparatives in its 2025 FS and used in BRPT's Note 1d and 2024 segment note (Note 46, PDF p. 239), are 3,787,513, 3,054,980 and 155,083. The net-asset gap equals the profit gap (3,529), and BREN's 2025 FS have no restatement note. CAP's 2024 total assets differ in the same way between Note 1d (5,663,616, PDF p. 24) and Note 34 (5,658,866, PDF p. 221), which suggests Note 34's 2024 summaries were compiled from different, perhaps preliminary, figures. That is inference.</p></li><li><p>The eliminations column in Note 46 (assets +139,079, depreciation +9,339) is described as behaving like an amortising consolidation adjustment. BRPT's acquisition of BREN's predecessor was accounted for under common control (Note 1d), so the adjustment probably relates to another acquisition or to something other than an excess; the FS do not say.</p></li><li><p>The Indonesian PSAK 227 text itself was not opened; paras. 10 and 12 were checked against the IAS 27 text.</p></li><li><p>The Islamic-finance mapping step in section 9 rests on knowledge of PSAK 401's presentation, not on a checked rule for consolidation.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Barito Pacific Tbk dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2025 (audited, USD thousand): Note 1d (PDF pp. 24, 26, 52); Note 3c (PDF pp. 67–68); Note 3e (PDF p. 70); Note 34 (PDF pp. 220–222); Note 46 (PDF p. 239). BRPT FS. PDF pages verified against the PDF text layer.</p></li><li><p>PT Barito Renewables Energy Tbk dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2025 and 2024 (audited, USD thousand) (statement of financial position PDF p. 12; Note 19a–b, PDF pp. 114–115) and BREN FS 2024. BRPT FS (subsidiary).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 110 <em>Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian</em> (IFRS 10): B86, B87, B88, B92, B93 VERIFIED (web, IFRS 10 text in Commission Regulation (EU) No 1254/2012, EUR-Lex); paras. 31–32 VERIFIED in the Long-Term Investments essay (EU Regulation 2016/1703).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 227 <em>Laporan Keuangan Tersendiri</em> (IAS 27), paras. 10 and 12: VERIFIED (web, IAS 27 as amended in Commission Regulation (EU) 2015/2441, checked by the independent recheck; BDO IFR Bulletin 2014/14). Amendment to PSAK 4 permitting the equity method, ratified 18 November 2015, effective for periods from 1 January 2016: VERIFIED (web, IAI exposure draft 7 Sep 2015; Binus accounting summary).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 103 (IFRS 3) para. 19 NCI measurement choice: VERIFIED in essay 25. PSAK 111 (para. 23), 201, 221, 240, 401: numbers from the IAI renumbering rule and list; content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Beams, Anthony, Bettinghaus and Smith, <em>Advanced Accounting</em>, chs. 3–4. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consolidated statements are never recorded in anyone's ledger. They are built on a worksheet that adds the parent's and the subsidiaries' statements line by line and then removes what is internal to the group: the parent's investment against the subsidiary's equity, intragroup balances, and intragroup income, expenses and dividends (PSAK 110 B86).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The elimination entries run in a fixed order. Reverse the parent's income from the subsidiary and the subsidiary's dividends, record the non-controlling interest's (NCI's) share, eliminate opening equity against the investment and opening NCI, allocate and depreciate the excess paid over book value, and cancel reciprocal balances. The worksheet has three parts, profit or loss, then retained earnings, then the balance sheet, each feeding the next.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The method the parent uses in its own books (cost or equity method, both allowed by PSAK 227) changes the first entry and adds one conversion entry, but never the consolidated result. A worksheet that balances can still be wrong, so the ties to the parent's equity and to NCI matter more than the totals.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Barito Pacific Tbk and subsidiaries (BRPT) list each subsidiary's \"total assets before elimination\" in their 2025 statements. PT Barito Renewables Energy Tbk (BREN), 64.67 percent owned, has 3,867,410 at 31 December 2025, in USD thousand (BRPT FS 2025, Note 1d, PDF p. 26). What happens between such \"before elimination\" figures and the group's statements is the subject of this page.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading it a student should be able to prepare a three-part consolidation worksheet in the year of acquisition and in later years, write each elimination entry in order, adapt the worksheet to a parent that uses the cost method, and find the error when the worksheet does not balance.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from BRPT's and BREN's statements are cited to their note; every other number is an illustration. Control, NCI measurement and profit attribution are taught in the ","type":"text"},{"text":"Non-Controlling Interest essay","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/non-controlling-interest","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":" and used here without re-deriving them.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. The parent's own books decide what must be eliminated","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The parent's investment in a subsidiary sits in its own books as one asset. How that asset is measured in the parent's ","type":"text"},{"text":"separate financial statements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (the parent's own statements, in which subsidiaries are investments) is a policy choice. PSAK 227 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Separate Financial Statements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 4, IAS 27) allows cost, PSAK 109 or the equity method, applied to each category of investment consistently (para. 10). The equity-method option was added in Indonesia by the amendment to PSAK 4 ratified in November 2015 and effective from 1 January 2016, following the 2014 IAS 27 amendment. Dividends from a subsidiary go to profit or loss under cost or PSAK 109, and reduce the investment under the equity method (para. 12).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"This page assumes the parent uses the complete equity method. The parent books its share of the subsidiary's profit after the depreciation of the excess it paid over book value, so its profit equals the consolidated profit attributable to its owners. Beams calls this ","type":"text"},{"text":"one-line consolidation","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"; the ","type":"text"},{"text":"Long-Term Investments essay","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":" (section 5) explains the mechanism.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The running illustration.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" BRPT holds 64.67 percent of BREN (Note 1d). Its interest in BREN's geothermal business came through common-control transactions. BRPT acquired Star Energy Group Holdings (SEGHPL) in 2018, pooled as if combined from the start (Note 1d, PDF p. 52) at book values (policy Note 3e, PDF p. 70), and BREN acquired SEGHPL from BRPT in 2022 for new BREN shares (BREN FS 2025, Note 19b, PDF p. 115). No fair value excess arose at BRPT's level, although goodwill from Star Energy's earlier purchase of Salak and Darajat was carried across at its carrying amount; the Business Combinations essay (K1) covers both. To show every entry, suppose instead (illustration) that on 1 January 2024 BRPT had bought 65 percent of a geothermal company from outside owners. The subsidiary is called Sub. A ","type":"text"},{"text":"fair value uplift","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is the amount by which an asset's fair value at acquisition exceeds its carrying amount in the subsidiary's books.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consideration paid = 520,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sub's book equity = share capital 400,000 + retained earnings 200,000 = 600,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fair value uplifts: power plant +100,000 (20 years left, so 5,000 a year); spare-parts inventory +10,000 (used in 2024)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Identifiable net assets at fair value = 600,000 + 100,000 + 10,000 = 710,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI at its proportionate share = 35% × 710,000 = 248,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill = 520,000 + 248,500 − 710,000 = 58,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Excess over book value = 520,000 + 248,500 − 600,000 = 168,500 = 100,000 + 10,000 + 58,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 103 lets the acquirer choose fair value or the proportionate share for NCI, deal by deal (essay 25, section 2). The proportionate share is used here. Deferred tax on the uplifts is ignored to keep the steps short; K1 adds it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sub earns 90,000 and pays dividends of 40,000 in 2024, then earns 110,000 and declares dividends of 50,000 in 2025, of which 20,000 is still unpaid at year end.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024 profit as the group sees it = 90,000 − 10,000 inventory − 5,000 plant = 75,000; parent 65% = 48,750; NCI 35% = 26,250","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025 profit as the group sees it = 110,000 − 5,000 plant = 105,000; parent 65% = 68,250; NCI 35% = 36,750","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Parent's books","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost method","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Equity method","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1 Jan 2024, purchase","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Investment in Sub 520,000 / Cr Cash 520,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"same","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024 profit of Sub","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no entry","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Investment in Sub 48,750 / Cr Income from Sub 48,750","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024 dividend, 65% × 40,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 26,000 / Cr Dividend income 26,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 26,000 / Cr Investment in Sub 26,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025 profit of Sub","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no entry","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Investment in Sub 68,250 / Cr Income from Sub 68,250","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025 dividend, 65% × 50,000 = 32,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 19,500, Dr Dividend receivable 13,000 / Cr Dividend income 32,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 19,500, Dr Dividend receivable 13,000 / Cr Investment in Sub 32,500","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Investment at 31 Dec 2025","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"520,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"520,000 + 48,750 − 26,000 + 68,250 − 32,500 = 578,500","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The equity-method balance already contains the parent's share of Sub's retained profits and of the depreciated uplift. The cost-method balance contains neither, so the worksheet has to put them there before it can eliminate anything (section 6).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. The worksheet has three parts, and each feeds the next","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Each part of the worksheet ends in the figure the next part needs. The worksheet stacks three parts (income statement, retained earnings statement, balance sheet), with columns for the parent, the subsidiary, the eliminations and the consolidated result.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Profit attributable to the owners of the parent, the last line of part one, is carried into part two.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Closing retained earnings, the last line of part two, is carried into part three.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Part three must then balance, total assets against total liabilities and equity.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 201 replaced the retained earnings statement with a statement of changes in equity, but the logic is unchanged. Every year starts again from the separate statements and repeats the entries for past events, such as the acquisition-date allocation.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 110 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Consolidated Financial Statements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10) describes the three steps the entries perform (B86): combine like items of assets, liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows; offset the parent's investment against its portion of the subsidiary's equity; and eliminate intragroup assets, liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows in full. BRPT's policy repeats the third step: \"All intragroup assets and liabilities, equity, income, expenses and cash flows relating to transactions between members of the Group are eliminated in full on consolidation\" (Note 3c, PDF p. 68).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. The standard elimination sequence","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"There are six entries, made in a fixed order. Each removes something that would otherwise be counted twice.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Step","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Problem it solves","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Entry (equity-method parent)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(1) Income from subsidiary and dividends","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The parent's income line repeats Sub's revenues and expenses; dividends to the parent move cash inside the group","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Income from Sub / Cr Dividends declared (Sub, parent's share); Cr Investment for the difference","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(2) NCI share","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consolidated profit belongs partly to outside owners, and their dividends reduce their claim","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr NCI share of profit / Cr Dividends declared (NCI's share); Cr NCI for the difference","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(3) Opening equity against investment and NCI","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sub's opening equity is already represented by the investment and opening NCI","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Share capital and opening retained earnings (Sub), Dr Unamortised excess / Cr Investment (opening), Cr NCI (opening)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(4) Allocate the excess","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The ","type":"text"},{"text":"unamortised excess","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is a worksheet suspense account to be assigned to the assets it paid for","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Plant and equipment, Dr Goodwill (and inventory or cost of sales) / Cr Unamortised excess","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(5) Depreciate the excess","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Group depreciation follows acquisition-date fair values (PSAK 110 B88); Sub depreciates book values","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Depreciation / Cr Plant and equipment","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(6) Reciprocal balances","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Balances between group companies are neither assets nor liabilities of the group","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr the payable / Cr the receivable; Dr intragroup income / Cr intragroup expense","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Entries (1) and (2) together remove all of Sub's dividends. Entry (1) returns the investment to its opening balance, which is why entry (3) can eliminate it against opening equity. The intragroup profit eliminations of K3 and K4 can sit anywhere in the sequence without changing the result.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. The year of acquisition","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In the first year the opening equity in entry (3) is the equity at the acquisition date, and the inventory uplift reaches cost of sales because the spares were used during the year. For example (illustration), the 2024 worksheet for Sub:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(1) Dr Income from Sub 48,750 / Cr Dividends declared 26,000; Cr Investment in Sub 22,750","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(2) Dr NCI share of profit 26,250 / Cr Dividends declared 14,000; Cr NCI 12,250","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(3) Dr Share capital, Sub 400,000; Dr Retained earnings, Sub 200,000; Dr Unamortised excess 168,500 / Cr Investment in Sub 520,000; Cr NCI 248,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(4) Dr Cost of sales 10,000; Dr Plant and equipment 100,000; Dr Goodwill 58,500 / Cr Unamortised excess 168,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(5) Dr Depreciation 5,000 / Cr Plant and equipment 5,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Entry (3) balances at 768,500 on each side. The investment, 520,000 + 48,750 − 26,000 = 542,750, is credited 22,750 + 520,000 = 542,750 and falls to zero. NCI closes at 248,500 + 12,250 = 260,750, which is 35 percent of Sub's net assets at group carrying amounts (book values plus the uplifts not yet written off).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"35% × (600,000 + 90,000 − 40,000 + 100,000 − 5,000) = 35% × 745,000 = 260,750","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under the proportionate method goodwill is not part of NCI. Had control been obtained during the year, Sub's income and expenses would enter only from the acquisition date (B88), a case K5 takes up.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. A later year: the full worksheet","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"From the second year on, entry (3) uses Sub's retained earnings at the start of the year and the excess still unamortised at that date. In 2025 three intragroup transactions also need entry (6).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"On 1 July 2025 the parent lent Sub 30,000 at 6 percent a year. Half a year's interest, 30,000 × 6% × 6/12 = 900, is accrued by both and unpaid.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"On 30 December Sub repaid 5,000 of the loan by bank transfer, which reached the parent on 2 January. Sub shows a loan payable of 25,000, while the parent still shows a receivable of 30,000. The 5,000 is ","type":"text"},{"text":"cash in transit","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", money that has left one group company and not yet reached the other.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sub's unpaid dividend of 20,000 includes 13,000 owed to the parent.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), the opening amounts for 2025:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sub retained earnings, 1 Jan 2025 = 200,000 + 90,000 − 40,000 = 250,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Unamortised excess, 1 Jan 2025 = 168,500 − 10,000 − 5,000 = 153,500 (plant 95,000 + goodwill 58,500)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Investment, 1 Jan 2025 = 542,750; NCI, 1 Jan 2025 = 260,750","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The entries:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(1) Dr Income from Sub 68,250 / Cr Dividends declared 32,500; Cr Investment in Sub 35,750","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(2) Dr NCI share of profit 36,750 / Cr Dividends declared 17,500; Cr NCI 19,250","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(3) Dr Share capital, Sub 400,000; Dr Retained earnings 1 Jan, Sub 250,000; Dr Unamortised excess 153,500 / Cr Investment in Sub 542,750; Cr NCI 260,750","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(4) Dr Plant and equipment 95,000; Dr Goodwill 58,500 / Cr Unamortised excess 153,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(5) Dr Depreciation 5,000 / Cr Plant and equipment 5,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(6a) Dr Cash in transit 5,000 / Cr Loan to Sub 5,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(6b) Dr Loan from parent 25,000 / Cr Loan to Sub 25,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(6c) Dr Interest payable 900 / Cr Interest receivable 900","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(6d) Dr Interest income 900 / Cr Finance costs 900","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(6e) Dr Dividend payable 13,000 / Cr Dividend receivable 13,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Entry (6a) is an adjustment, not an elimination; the reciprocal balances must agree before (6b) cancels them. A cheque in transit is handled the same way, with an adjustment such as \"Cash 20,000 / Note receivable – Sub 20,000\" ahead of the eliminations.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The worksheet, Parent and Sub, year ended 31 December 2025 (illustration).","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Credit balances in the income statement and equity are shown positive, expenses and dividends in parentheses; the elimination columns show the entry number.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Parent","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sub","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consolidated","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":nu