-- decommissioning-provision-psak-237-and-contingencies
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>A provision is a liability of uncertain timing or amount. It is recognised when a past event has left a present obligation the entity cannot realistically avoid, an outflow is more likely than not, and the amount can be estimated. Anything weaker is a contingent liability, disclosed unless remote.</p></li><li><p>A provision is measured at the best estimate of what it would rationally cost to settle the obligation today. That is an expected value over a large population or the most likely outcome adjusted for skew for a single obligation, discounted at a pre-tax rate that matches the cash flows (nominal with nominal, real with real).</p></li><li><p>A decommissioning obligation is a provision whose cost is added to the asset that caused it. The discount unwinds as a finance cost, and changes in the estimate or the rate move the asset, not profit, until the asset is used up. PHE carries 3,826,222 of it, its largest liability.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report a provision for decommissioning and site restoration of 3,826,222 at 31 December 2024 against 3,784,917 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; "Daftar" is the printed page). It is the largest liability line, 25.0 percent of total liabilities and 12.6 percent of total assets (22.7 and 12.3 percent a year earlier). This page also covers other non-current liabilities, 281,228 (273,774 in 2023).</p><p>A student should come away able to tell a provision from a contingent liability, measure it, journal a decommissioning provision through its life, and see where other standards take over.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.</p><h2>1. What a provision is</h2><p>A provision is a liability whose timing or amount is uncertain (PSAK 237.10; formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37). It is still a liability, a present obligation from a past event settled by an outflow of resources. The uncertainty sets it apart from other liabilities (para. 11).</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Trade payables</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Accruals</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Provisions</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Goods or services</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>received</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>received</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>often none involved</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Amount and date</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>invoiced or formally agreed</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>estimated, with little uncertainty</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>uncertain in timing or amount</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PHE example, 2024</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>trade payables 1,091,822</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>accrued expenses 1,897,743</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>this line, 3,826,222</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Allowances against assets, such as PHE's "provision for impairment of non-financial assets", are not PSAK 237 provisions (para. 7). A provision is current or non-current by expected settlement date; PHE presents this one as wholly non-current.</p><h2>2. Recognition</h2><p>A provision is recognised only when three conditions hold together (para. 14): a present obligation, legal or constructive, from a past event; a probable outflow of resources; and a reliable estimate.</p><p><strong>Present obligation from a past event.</strong> The past event is the <em>obligating event</em>, one that leaves the entity no realistic alternative to settling (para. 17). A <em>legal obligation</em> comes from contract or law; a <em>constructive obligation</em> from the entity's own policy or practice that has created a valid expectation in others (para. 10).</p><p>The test that does most of the work is whether the obligation exists independently of the entity's future actions (para. 19). The standard's own example is upstream. Decommissioning an oil installation is provided for to the extent damage has already been done, while a cost the entity could avoid by operating differently, such as fitting new filters, is not. For example (illustration), a subsidiary that has drilled 12 wells and plans 8 more provides for plugging 12; the 8 could still go undrilled.</p><p>The same logic rules out provisions for future operating losses (para. 63); an expected loss is an impairment indicator instead (para. 65).</p><p><strong>Probable outflow.</strong> Probable means more likely than not, a probability above 50 percent (para. 23). For many similar obligations, such as warranties, the test is applied to the class as a whole (para. 24).</p><p><strong>Reliable estimate.</strong> The standard expects a range of outcomes to be estimable in all but extremely rare cases (para. 25). Where none can be made, the liability is disclosed as a contingent liability (para. 26).</p><h2>3. Measurement</h2><p>The amount recognised is the <em>best estimate</em> of the expenditure required to settle the obligation at the reporting date (para. 36). That is the amount the entity would rationally pay to settle it, or to transfer it to a third party, today (para. 37).</p><p><strong>Expected value for a large population.</strong> When the provision covers many items, outcomes are weighted by their probabilities (para. 39). For example (illustration), a subsidiary must plug 40 old onshore wells. Experience says 70 percent need a simple cement plug at 400 each, 25 percent need remedial cementing at 700, and 5 percent have stuck pipe that must be fished out at 1,600.</p><blockquote><p>Expected cost per well = 70% × 400 + 25% × 700 + 5% × 1,600 = 280 + 175 + 80 = 535</p><p>Provision, before discounting = 40 × 535 = 21,400</p></blockquote><p><strong>Most likely outcome for a single obligation.</strong> One platform has one outcome, so the starting point is the most likely one. Other outcomes still count. If they are mostly higher, the best estimate is higher (para. 40). For example (illustration), removing one platform will most likely cost 30,000 (60 percent), but there is a 30 percent chance of 45,000 if the jacket is corroded and a 10 percent chance of 25,000 if a heavy-lift vessel is already in the area.</p><blockquote><p>Most likely outcome = 30,000; probability-weighted outcome = 60% × 30,000 + 30% × 45,000 + 10% × 25,000 = 34,000</p></blockquote><p>The skew is upward, so the best estimate lies above 30,000.</p><p><strong>Risk.</strong> Uncertainty is reflected once, in the cash flows or the rate, never both (paras. 42–43 and 47).</p><p><strong>Present value.</strong> Where timing matters, the provision is the present value of the expected outflows, at a pre-tax rate reflecting current market assessments of the time value of money and the risks specific to the liability (paras. 45–47). Cash flows and rate must match. Suppose the 34,000 is in today's prices, the work happens in 10 years, cost inflation is 2.5 percent and the nominal rate is 5.0 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Nominal method: 34,000 × 1.025^10 = 43,523 in year-10 prices; 43,523 / 1.05^10 = 26,719</p><p>Real method: real rate = 1.05 / 1.025 − 1 = 2.439%; 34,000 / 1.02439^10 = 26,719</p><p>Mixed (today's prices at the nominal rate): 34,000 / 1.05^10 = 20,873, understated by 5,846</p></blockquote><p><strong>Future events, disposals and reimbursements.</strong> Expected technology savings count only on objective evidence, new legislation only when virtually certain (paras. 48–50), and scrap proceeds not at all (para. 51). A reimbursement, for example from an insurer, is a separate asset recognised only when virtually certain and capped at the provision (para. 53); only the expense may be shown net (para. 54).</p><h2>4. Common provisions</h2><p>Kieso lists six forms. Litigation, warranties, onerous contracts and restructuring follow, environmental obligations get section 5, and consideration payable has left PSAK 237.</p><p><strong>Litigation.</strong> A lawsuit needs a cause before the reporting date, a probable loss and an estimate; expert opinion is evidence (para. 16).</p><p><strong>Warranties.</strong> An <em>assurance-type warranty</em> promises that the product works as specified, so it is a cost of the sale, provided for when the sale is made. A <em>service-type warranty</em> sells extra cover, a separate performance obligation under PSAK 115 (see the Deferred revenue essay). For example (illustration), a seller sells 1,000 units at USD 500 each, expects warranty repairs on 100 units at USD 200 each (full amounts), pays 20 claims in the year and expects 80 next year.</p><blockquote><p>Paid in the year: Dr Warranty expense 4,000 / Cr Cash, inventories 4,000</p><p>31 December: Dr Warranty expense 16,000 / Cr Warranty liability 16,000 (80 × 200)</p></blockquote><p><em>Consideration payable</em> to customers, such as premiums and rebates, is now part of revenue under PSAK 115. It reduces the transaction price, and an expected refund is a refund liability (see the Deferred revenue essay).</p><p><strong>Onerous contracts.</strong> A contract is <em>onerous</em> when the unavoidable cost of meeting it exceeds the benefit expected from it (para. 10), and the unavoidable cost is the <em>least net cost of exiting</em>, the lower of the cost of fulfilling it net of what fulfilling still earns and the penalty for walking away (para. 68). Assets dedicated to the contract are tested for impairment first (para. 69). Since the 2020 amendment the cost of fulfilling includes both incremental costs and an allocation of other directly related costs. For example (illustration), a subsidiary has a drilling services contract costing 60,000 for 2025, and the programme is cancelled. Exiting costs a penalty of 25,000; re-selling the services to another operator would bring in 40,000.</p><blockquote><p>Cost of fulfilling, net of benefits = 60,000 − 40,000 = 20,000; penalty = 25,000</p><p>Provision = lower of the two = 20,000</p><p>Dr Loss on onerous contract 20,000 / Cr Provision for onerous contract 20,000</p></blockquote><p><strong>Restructuring.</strong> A constructive obligation needs a detailed formal plan, started or announced to those affected (para. 72). Only direct costs not associated with continuing activities are included, so retraining staff who stay, marketing and new systems are excluded (paras. 80–81).</p><h2>5. Decommissioning: the asset retirement obligation</h2><p>Installing a well, platform or pipeline creates an obligation to plug, remove and restore the site at the end of field life. PHE recognises it for "oil and gas production facilities, wells, pipelines and related assets in accordance with the provisions in the PSC or in line with applicable regulations" (Note 3o, Daftar 5/60, PDF p. 77). The Oil and gas properties essay shows the asset side; this section follows the liability.</p><p><strong>Initial recognition.</strong> The provision is recognised on installation and grows as more wells are drilled. The cost goes into the asset under PSAK 216.16(c) (formerly PSAK 16, IAS 16), because removing the platform is part of what it costs to use it. The removal cost has no future benefit of its own, so it is not a separate asset (following Kieso). Where the obligation arises from producing inventory during a period, as when a mine disturbs more land to extract this year's ore, the cost goes to inventory under PSAK 202 instead.</p><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary completes an offshore platform with 20 wells on 31 December 2024. Removal and plugging are expected at the end of 2036, 12 years later. In today's prices the engineers see a 60 percent chance of 80,000, 30 percent of 100,000 and 10 percent of 140,000. Cost inflation is 2.5 percent and the pre-tax nominal discount rate 5.0 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Expected cost in today's prices = 60% × 80,000 + 30% × 100,000 + 10% × 140,000 = 92,000</p><p>In 2036 prices = 92,000 × 1.025^12 = 123,730</p><p>Present value at 31 December 2024 = 123,730 / 1.05^12 = 68,897</p></blockquote><p>One platform is a single obligation, so 80,000 is the most likely outcome. The other outcomes are all higher, so the best estimate lies above it, and this page uses the weighted 92,000, as section 3 allowed.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Oil and gas properties 68,897 / Cr Provision for decommissioning 68,897</p></blockquote><p><strong>Unwinding of the discount.</strong> As settlement approaches, the present value rises by the discount rate. The increase is a <em>finance cost</em> (para. 60) that ISAK 101 (formerly ISAK 9, IFRIC 1) sends to profit or loss and forbids capitalising. PHE calls it accretion.</p><blockquote><p>2025: 68,897 × 5% = 3,445; provision = 72,342</p><p>2026: 72,342 × 5% = 3,617; provision = 75,959</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Dr Finance cost 3,445 / Cr Provision for decommissioning 3,445 (2026: 3,617)</p></blockquote><p><strong>Changes in estimate.</strong> The expected cash flows, their timing and the discount rate all move the provision. Under ISAK 101, for an asset carried at cost, the change is added to or deducted from the asset and depleted over its remaining life. A deduction may not exceed the asset's carrying amount, and any excess goes to profit. An addition is considered as a possible impairment indicator and, if it is one, the asset is tested. Once the asset reaches the end of its useful life, every later change goes to profit or loss. PHE's policy states the add/deduct rule, the cap and the impairment check (Note 3o).</p><p>Suppose that at the end of 2026 the market rate for this liability falls to 4.0 percent, with 10 years to go and the cash estimate unchanged.</p><blockquote><p>Remeasured provision = 123,730 / 1.04^10 = 83,588; increase = 83,588 − 75,959 = 7,629</p><p>Dr Oil and gas properties 7,629 / Cr Provision for decommissioning 7,629</p></blockquote><p>From 2027 the provision unwinds at 4.0 percent. Now suppose that at the end of 2033, with three years left, a cheaper plugging method cuts the 2036 estimate by 12,000, and the platform's carrying amount is down to 6,000.</p><blockquote><p>Provision before the change = 123,730 / 1.04^3 = 109,996</p><p>Decrease = 12,000 / 1.04^3 = 10,668; new provision = 99,328</p><p>Dr Provision for decommissioning 10,668 / Cr Oil and gas properties 6,000 / Cr Gain on decommissioning estimate 4,668</p></blockquote><p><strong>Settlement.</strong> The provision is used only for its original purpose (para. 61). By the end of 2036 the provision has unwound to the revised nominal estimate of 123,730 − 12,000 = 111,730. If the contractor charges 115,000, the 3,270 difference is a loss of that period.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Provision for decommissioning 111,730, Dr Loss on settlement 3,270 / Cr Cash 115,000</p></blockquote><p><strong>Sensitivity.</strong> A long-dated provision moves sharply with its discount rate. Take PHE's 2024 balance as the starting point, and treat it, for illustration only, as one payment 15 years away at 3.17 percent, the ratio of PHE's 2024 accretion to its opening balance (Note 29).</p><blockquote><p>Implied payment in 15 years = 3,826,222 × 1.0317^15 = 6,110,428</p><p>At 4.17%: 6,110,428 / 1.0417^15 = 3,310,790, a fall of 515,432 (13.5%)</p><p>At 2.17%: 6,110,428 / 1.0217^15 = 4,428,134, a rise of 601,912 (15.7%)</p></blockquote><p>The rise exceeds the fall because present value is convex in the rate. Under ISAK 101 most of either swing would land on oil and gas properties, not profit.</p><h2>6. Contingent liabilities and contingent assets</h2><p>A <em>contingent liability</em> is either a possible obligation whose existence will be confirmed only by uncertain future events outside the entity's control, or a present obligation that fails the probability or estimate test (para. 10). It is never recognised (para. 27). It is disclosed, with an estimate of its effect where practicable, unless remote (paras. 28 and 86), and becomes a provision in the period the outflow turns probable (para. 30).</p><p><strong>Joint and several liability.</strong> The part of a joint obligation expected to be met by the others is a contingent liability, and the entity provides only for its own probable share (para. 29). For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary holds 30 percent of a block whose partners are jointly and severally liable for a 10,000 remediation order.</p><blockquote><p>Provision = 30% × 10,000 = 3,000; contingent liability disclosed = 7,000, unless the partners look unable to pay</p></blockquote><p>PHE discloses a real case. A 2022 ICC award orders Pertamina E&amp;P Libya and Pertamina, jointly and severally, to pay Libya's national oil company 24,000 and 5,200 plus interest for unfulfilled exploration commitments (Note 45e(i), Daftar 5/197–5/198, PDF pp. 214–215).</p><p>PHE is also guarantor for a former Australian subsidiary's share of abandonment costs in the Basker Manta Gummy block. The operator sued in Victoria; PHE's position, on legal advice, is that it owes nothing because abandonment was not completed within five years of withdrawal (Note 45e(ii), Daftar 5/198–5/202, PDF pp. 215–219). The note discloses no amount and does not say whether a provision is held. Its place under Note 45, "commitments and contingencies", with no matching provision in Notes 28 or 29, is consistent with a contingent liability.</p><p>A <em>contingent asset</em> is a possible asset from past events, such as a claim being pursued in court (paras. 10 and 32). It is not recognised (paras. 31 and 33), is disclosed when an inflow is probable (para. 34), and is recognised only when virtually certain (para. 35). A liability is booked at more likely than not, an asset only at virtually certain. For example (illustration), an insurance claim of 5,000 disputed at year end is disclosed; when the insurer accepts it in writing next year, it is booked.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Insurance claim receivable 5,000 / Cr Other income 5,000</p></blockquote><h2>7. Decommissioning funds and the other non-current liabilities</h2><p><strong>Funds set aside.</strong> Under IFRIC 5 a contributor to a decommissioning fund keeps its obligation as a liability and recognises its interest in the fund separately; without control or influence over the fund, the reimbursement right is measured at the lower of the provision and its share of the fair value of the fund's net assets. IAI's renumbering list has no ISAK for IFRIC 5, but PSAK 237.53 and the offsetting ban in PSAK 201 (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1) give the same gross answer. For example (illustration), a provision of 10,000 and a fund of 6,000 appear as a liability of 10,000 and an asset of 6,000, never a net liability of 4,000.</p><p>PHE's funds sit in joint accounts with SKK Migas, usable only for restoration with its approval, and are presented as restricted cash (Note 3o, Daftar 5/61, PDF p. 78), 1,890,493 non-current at the end of 2024 (Note 7, Daftar 5/93–5/95, PDF pp. 110–112), plus its share of joint-operation funds, 66,719, in other non-current assets (Note 14b, Daftar 5/105, PDF p. 122). Their fall from 296,555 is mostly a transfer into PHR's joint account (p. 123).</p><p><strong>Other non-current liabilities.</strong> PHE's Note 28 shows why a liability with "contingent" in its name need not be a PSAK 237 item (Daftar 5/144, PDF p. 161).</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2023</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Governing standard</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Acquisition of the Masela block</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>167,902</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>159,311</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 103, contingent consideration</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Penalty for unfulfilled work commitments (Libya)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>39,600</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>39,600</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>liability; possibly the ICC award in Note 45e(i), not stated</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Monitoring and recovery of two wells</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>25,948</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>25,959</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSAK 237, decommissioning-type</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Utilisation of the Badak LNG plant</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>18,313</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>22,503</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>obligation under PHI's WBX-NR sales contract; standard not stated</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PSC partner</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>10,387</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>9,100</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>not explained</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Others</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>19,078</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>17,312</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>includes Simenggaris sunk cost 9,720 and abandonment costs 7,530</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>281,228</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>273,774</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>PHE Masela bought a 20 percent participating interest from Shell, completed on 18 October 2023 (Note 5f, Daftar 5/88, PDF p. 105), and owes a further payment if the block's partners take a final investment decision (FID). Under PSAK 103 <em>Business Combinations</em> (formerly PSAK 22, IFRS 3) contingent consideration is part of the price, recognised at fair value at the acquisition date (PHE measures it at present value under PSAK 113, Note 28) and, if it is a liability, remeasured at fair value through profit or loss (Note 3d, Daftar 5/36, PDF p. 53). PHE applies PSAK 103 to this acquired interest in a joint operation, consistent with PSAK 111's rule for interests that are businesses. PSAK 237 does not apply, both because another standard covers it (para. 5) and because a contractual obligation to pay cash is a financial liability (para. 2).</p><p>The two standards treat the same odds differently. For example (illustration), an acquirer will pay 100,000 if FID is taken in four years, judged 40 percent likely, with a discount rate of 8 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Under PSAK 237 logic: outflow 40% &lt; 50%, nothing recognised, contingent liability disclosed</p><p>Under PSAK 103: fair value = 40% × 100,000 / 1.08^4 = 29,401, recognised as part of the cost of the acquisition</p><p>A year later the odds rise to 60%: 60% × 100,000 / 1.08^3 = 47,630; Dr Loss on remeasurement 18,229 / Cr Contingent consideration liability 18,229</p></blockquote><h2>8. Provisions across industries</h2><p><strong>Mining.</strong> Indonesian miners provide for reclamation and post-mining closure under PP 78/2010 and also place guarantees, such as time deposits, that do not discharge the obligation, so both are shown gross. Power plants carry the same provision. Mining also tests section 5's asset-or-inventory split every year. For example (illustration), a coal pit incurs a restoration obligation of 2,000 for land disturbed to extract this year's coal and 3,000 for a haul road built to serve the mine for ten years.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Inventories, coal 2,000 / Cr Provision for reclamation 2,000</p><p>Dr Mine properties 3,000 / Cr Provision for reclamation 3,000</p></blockquote><p><strong>Lessees and telecom towers.</strong> Restoring leased land puts the cost into the right-of-use asset and the obligation into a PSAK 237 provision (see the Right-of-use essay).</p><p><strong>Levies.</strong> ISAK 121 <em>Levies</em> (formerly ISAK 30, IFRIC 21) makes the obligating event the activity that triggers payment under the law, not the period the levy relates to. For example (illustration), a levy of 1,200 for a year that is triggered by being in operation on 1 January is a full liability of 1,200 on 1 January, not 100 a month.</p><p><strong>Banks and insurers.</strong> Financial guarantees and loan commitments fall under PSAK 109 (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9), with expected credit losses, not under PSAK 237 (para. 2). Insurers' claims liabilities fall under PSAK 117 (formerly PSAK 74, IFRS 17).</p><h2>9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 29</h2><p>PSAK 237 asks for a reconciliation showing additions, amounts used, unused amounts reversed and the unwinding (para. 84), and for the expected timing, uncertainties, major assumptions and reimbursements (para. 85). PHE gives a three-line movement (Note 29, Daftar 5/145, PDF p. 162).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Balance at beginning of year</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,784,917</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,460,330</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Additions/(deductions) and revisions of estimates</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>78,620</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>211,366</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Accretion expense</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>119,925</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>113,221</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Balance at end of year</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>3,826,222</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>3,784,917</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>2023: 3,460,330 + 211,366 + 113,221 = 3,784,917</p><p>2024: 3,784,917 + 78,620 + 119,925 = 3,983,462, not 3,826,222; 3,784,917 − 78,620 + 119,925 = 3,826,222</p></blockquote><p>The 2024 revisions only foot as a deduction of 78,620, though they are printed without brackets. Alternatively PHE omitted a line: if 78,620 is an addition, an unprinted reduction of 157,240 is needed. The Oil and gas properties essay gives the same two readings. Three more points stand out.</p><ul><li><p>There is no line for amounts used. Either no abandonment spending was charged in two years, or it is netted inside the revisions, though paragraph 84 asks for it separately.</p></li><li><p>Note 51 reports non-cash decommissioning adjustments to oil and gas properties of +12,627 in 2024 and 202,685 in 2023 (Daftar 5/235, PDF p. 252), against Note 29 revisions of 78,620 (a deduction on the footing reading) and 211,366. The gaps of 91,247 and 8,681 could be utilisation, revisions taken to profit, disposals or exchange differences; the FS do not say.</p></li><li><p>The discount rate, inflation assumption and timing are not disclosed. Note 29 describes ARO as the present value of costs "expected to be incurred during the terms of the PSCs" and Note 4b(ii) lists the main uncertainties (Daftar 5/80, PDF p. 97).</p></li></ul><blockquote><p>Accretion / opening provision = 119,925 / 3,784,917 = 3.17% (2023: 113,221 / 3,460,330 = 3.27%)</p><p>Accretion / finance cost = 119,925 / 453,906 = 26.4% (Note 41, Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167)</p><p>Funds held / provision = (1,890,493 + 66,719) / 3,826,222 = 51.2% (2023: 52.8%)</p><p>Deferred tax on the provision = 66,352 + 412,586 = 478,938, or 12.5% of the provision (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172)</p></blockquote><p>About half the obligation, at present value, is backed by cash usable for nothing else. The deferred tax ratio is far below PHE's tax rates, plausibly because ASR deposits are recoverable or deductible when placed (section 10), leaving much of the provision with no future deduction; the mechanics belong to the income tax essays. Note 28's 2023 lines also add to 273,785, not the 273,774 printed, a difference of 11.</p><h2>10. Upstream note: abandonment and site restoration under the PSC</h2><p>Indonesian upstream contractors set aside abandonment and site restoration (ASR) funds under Minister of Energy and Mineral Resources Regulation 15/2018, starting in the year production becomes commercial, in a joint account with SKK Migas at a state-owned bank. Under cost recovery the amounts set aside are recoverable costs; under gross split they are deductible for income tax. PHE OSES, for example, deposited funds for both halves of 2024 on SKK Migas instruction (Note 45i, Daftar 5/208, PDF p. 225).</p><h2>11. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net">oil-gas-properties-net</a>: the asset that absorbs the initial estimate and later revisions.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/restricted-cash">restricted-cash-non-current</a>: the ASR funds.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current">other-non-current-assets</a>: funds in joint operations.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/finance-cost">finance-cost</a>: the accretion.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-liabilities</a>: the deductible temporary difference on the provision.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/trade-payables">trade-payables</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/accrued-expenses">accrued-expenses</a>: the liabilities with certain or nearly certain amounts.</p></li><li><p><code>other-non-current-liabilities</code>: covered here, section 7.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>That PHE treats the Basker Manta Gummy claim as a contingent liability is inferred from its place in Note 45 (commitments and contingencies) and the absence of an amount or provision. The item itself is not labelled.</p></li><li><p>Reading Note 29's 78,620 as a deduction rests on footing alone; PHE may instead have omitted a line of 157,240. The reasons for the gaps to Note 51 are not disclosed, and the listed causes are possibilities, not findings. That ASR deposits explain the low deferred tax ratio is this page's reading.</p></li><li><p>The 3.17 percent "rate" and the single 15-year payment in the sensitivity are illustrative simplifications. PHE discloses neither its discount rate nor the timing, and a portfolio of fields with different rates and dates would respond differently.</p></li><li><p>That Indonesia has no ISAK equivalent of IFRIC 5 rests on IAI's renumbering list, which lists only interpretations in force. Whether PHE's joint ASR accounts would fall within IFRIC 5's scope if it applied is this page's reading; PHE presents them as restricted cash.</p></li><li><p>The governing standards given for the Note 28 items are this page's reading of one-line descriptions. The Libya penalty may be the ICC award of Note 45e(i); the FS do not link them, and the balance shows no interest accrual. The 51.2 percent funding ratio assumes all non-current restricted cash is ASR funds, as Note 7's description implies but does not state line by line.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); Note 3d (Daftar 5/36, PDF p. 53); Note 3o (Daftar 5/60–5/61, PDF pp. 77–78); Note 4b(ii) (Daftar 5/80, PDF p. 97); Note 5f (Daftar 5/88, PDF p. 105); Note 7 (Daftar 5/93–5/95, PDF pp. 110–112); Note 14b (Daftar 5/105–5/106, PDF pp. 122–123); Note 28 (Daftar 5/144, PDF p. 161); Note 29 (Daftar 5/145, PDF p. 162); Note 41 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172); Note 45e(i)–(ii) (Daftar 5/197–5/202, PDF pp. 214–219); Note 45i (Daftar 5/208, PDF p. 225); Note 51 (Daftar 5/235, PDF p. 252). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk; text dump, Indonesian and English columns read).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 237 <em>Provisi, Liabilitas Kontinjensi, dan Aset Kontinjensi</em> (IAS 37), paras. 1–2, 5, 7, 10–35, 36–43, 45–55, 59–61, 63, 65–69, 72, 80–81, 84–86, 89; para. 5(f) on contingent consideration re-checked in the current consolidated text (faronline.se). VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation text of IAS 37 reproduced at ktkt.uel.edu.vn; that consolidation predates the 2020 amendment). The 2020 "Onerous Contracts — Cost of Fulfilling a Contract" amendment: VERIFIED in the Inventories essay (web, IFRS Foundation).</p></li><li><p>ISAK 101 (IFRIC 1 <em>Changes in Existing Decommissioning, Restoration and Similar Liabilities</em>): cost model, cap at carrying amount, impairment indicator, end of useful life, unwinding as finance cost and no capitalisation. VERIFIED (web, BDO New Zealand summary of NZ IFRIC 1, 2025). Paragraph numbers not cited.</p></li><li><p>IFRIC 5 <em>Rights to Interests arising from Decommissioning, Restoration and Environmental Rehabilitation Funds</em>: separate recognition and the lower-of measurement. VERIFIED (web, IAS Plus; BDO NZ summary). No ISAK 105 or other ISAK for IFRIC 5 in the list: VERIFIED (web, IAI, "Komparasi Perubahan Penomoran PSAK ISAK SAK Indonesia"; confirmed in the independent recheck). IFRIC 5.9 lower-of measurement: VERIFIED (web, secondary, BDO).</p></li><li><p>PSAK numbers PSAK 237 (57), PSAK 103 (22), PSAK 111 (66), PSAK 113 (68), PSAK 117 (74), ISAK 101 (ISAK 9), ISAK 121 (ISAK 30): VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list). PSAK 103 contingent consideration (paras. 39–40, 58), PSAK 111.21A (acquired interests in joint operations that are businesses), PSAK 216.16(c) and 18, PSAK 202, PSAK 109, PSAK 117, ISAK 121 content: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED here, except as quoted in PHE's Note 3d.</p></li><li><p>Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition, chapter on current liabilities, provisions and contingencies. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Peraturan Menteri ESDM No. 15/2018 on post-operation activities in upstream oil and gas: ASR funds, joint account with SKK Migas, cost recovery and gross split treatment. VERIFIED (primary: Berita Negara 2018 No. 305, Arts. 11(2)(b), 11(3), 12(1)–(2), checked in the independent recheck; also SSEK, Ditjen Migas).</p></li><li><p>PP No. 78/2010 on reclamation and post-mining, with Permen ESDM 26/2018: reclamation and post-mining guarantees. VERIFIED (web, secondary: BPK regional notes, law-firm summaries). Current amendments not checked.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A provision is a liability of uncertain timing or amount. It is recognised when a past event has left a present obligation the entity cannot realistically avoid, an outflow is more likely than not, and the amount can be estimated. Anything weaker is a contingent liability, disclosed unless remote.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A provision is measured at the best estimate of what it would rationally cost to settle the obligation today. That is an expected value over a large population or the most likely outcome adjusted for skew for a single obligation, discounted at a pre-tax rate that matches the cash flows (nominal with nominal, real with real).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A decommissioning obligation is a provision whose cost is added to the asset that caused it. The discount unwinds as a finance cost, and changes in the estimate or the rate move the asset, not profit, until the asset is used up. PHE carries 3,826,222 of it, its largest liability.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report a provision for decommissioning and site restoration of 3,826,222 at 31 December 2024 against 3,784,917 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). It is the largest liability line, 25.0 percent of total liabilities and 12.6 percent of total assets (22.7 and 12.3 percent a year earlier). This page also covers other non-current liabilities, 281,228 (273,774 in 2023).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A student should come away able to tell a provision from a contingent liability, measure it, journal a decommissioning provision through its life, and see where other standards take over.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What a provision is","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A provision is a liability whose timing or amount is uncertain (PSAK 237.10; formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37). It is still a liability, a present obligation from a past event settled by an outflow of resources. The uncertainty sets it apart from other liabilities (para. 11).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Trade payables","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accruals","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Provisions","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goods or services","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"received","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"received","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"often none involved","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Amount and date","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"invoiced or formally agreed","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"estimated, with little uncertainty","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"uncertain in timing or amount","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE example, 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade payables 1,091,822","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"accrued expenses 1,897,743","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"this line, 3,826,222","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Allowances against assets, such as PHE's \"provision for impairment of non-financial assets\", are not PSAK 237 provisions (para. 7). A provision is current or non-current by expected settlement date; PHE presents this one as wholly non-current.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Recognition","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A provision is recognised only when three conditions hold together (para. 14): a present obligation, legal or constructive, from a past event; a probable outflow of resources; and a reliable estimate.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Present obligation from a past event.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The past event is the ","type":"text"},{"text":"obligating event","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", one that leaves the entity no realistic alternative to settling (para. 17). A ","type":"text"},{"text":"legal obligation","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" comes from contract or law; a ","type":"text"},{"text":"constructive obligation","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" from the entity's own policy or practice that has created a valid expectation in others (para. 10).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The test that does most of the work is whether the obligation exists independently of the entity's future actions (para. 19). The standard's own example is upstream. Decommissioning an oil installation is provided for to the extent damage has already been done, while a cost the entity could avoid by operating differently, such as fitting new filters, is not. For example (illustration), a subsidiary that has drilled 12 wells and plans 8 more provides for plugging 12; the 8 could still go undrilled.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The same logic rules out provisions for future operating losses (para. 63); an expected loss is an impairment indicator instead (para. 65).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Probable outflow.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Probable means more likely than not, a probability above 50 percent (para. 23). For many similar obligations, such as warranties, the test is applied to the class as a whole (para. 24).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Reliable estimate.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The standard expects a range of outcomes to be estimable in all but extremely rare cases (para. 25). Where none can be made, the liability is disclosed as a contingent liability (para. 26).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Measurement","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The amount recognised is the ","type":"text"},{"text":"best estimate","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" of the expenditure required to settle the obligation at the reporting date (para. 36). That is the amount the entity would rationally pay to settle it, or to transfer it to a third party, today (para. 37).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expected value for a large population.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When the provision covers many items, outcomes are weighted by their probabilities (para. 39). For example (illustration), a subsidiary must plug 40 old onshore wells. Experience says 70 percent need a simple cement plug at 400 each, 25 percent need remedial cementing at 700, and 5 percent have stuck pipe that must be fished out at 1,600.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expected cost per well = 70% × 400 + 25% × 700 + 5% × 1,600 = 280 + 175 + 80 = 535","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Provision, before discounting = 40 × 535 = 21,400","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Most likely outcome for a single obligation.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" One platform has one outcome, so the starting point is the most likely one. Other outcomes still count. If they are mostly higher, the best estimate is higher (para. 40). For example (illustration), removing one platform will most likely cost 30,000 (60 percent), but there is a 30 percent chance of 45,000 if the jacket is corroded and a 10 percent chance of 25,000 if a heavy-lift vessel is already in the area.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Most likely outcome = 30,000; probability-weighted outcome = 60% × 30,000 + 30% × 45,000 + 10% × 25,000 = 34,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The skew is upward, so the best estimate lies above 30,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Risk.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Uncertainty is reflected once, in the cash flows or the rate, never both (paras. 42–43 and 47).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Present value.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Where timing matters, the provision is the present value of the expected outflows, at a pre-tax rate reflecting current market assessments of the time value of money and the risks specific to the liability (paras. 45–47). Cash flows and rate must match. Suppose the 34,000 is in today's prices, the work happens in 10 years, cost inflation is 2.5 percent and the nominal rate is 5.0 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Nominal method: 34,000 × 1.025^10 = 43,523 in year-10 prices; 43,523 / 1.05^10 = 26,719","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Real method: real rate = 1.05 / 1.025 − 1 = 2.439%; 34,000 / 1.02439^10 = 26,719","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Mixed (today's prices at the nominal rate): 34,000 / 1.05^10 = 20,873, understated by 5,846","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Future events, disposals and reimbursements.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Expected technology savings count only on objective evidence, new legislation only when virtually certain (paras. 48–50), and scrap proceeds not at all (para. 51). A reimbursement, for example from an insurer, is a separate asset recognised only when virtually certain and capped at the provision (para. 53); only the expense may be shown net (para. 54).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Common provisions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso lists six forms. Litigation, warranties, onerous contracts and restructuring follow, environmental obligations get section 5, and consideration payable has left PSAK 237.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Litigation.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A lawsuit needs a cause before the reporting date, a probable loss and an estimate; expert opinion is evidence (para. 16).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Warranties.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" An ","type":"text"},{"text":"assurance-type warranty","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" promises that the product works as specified, so it is a cost of the sale, provided for when the sale is made. A ","type":"text"},{"text":"service-type warranty","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" sells extra cover, a separate performance obligation under PSAK 115 (see the Deferred revenue essay). For example (illustration), a seller sells 1,000 units at USD 500 each, expects warranty repairs on 100 units at USD 200 each (full amounts), pays 20 claims in the year and expects 80 next year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid in the year: Dr Warranty expense 4,000 / Cr Cash, inventories 4,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 December: Dr Warranty expense 16,000 / Cr Warranty liability 16,000 (80 × 200)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consideration payable","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" to customers, such as premiums and rebates, is now part of revenue under PSAK 115. It reduces the transaction price, and an expected refund is a refund liability (see the Deferred revenue essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Onerous contracts.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A contract is ","type":"text"},{"text":"onerous","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" when the unavoidable cost of meeting it exceeds the benefit expected from it (para. 10), and the unavoidable cost is the ","type":"text"},{"text":"least net cost of exiting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the lower of the cost of fulfilling it net of what fulfilling still earns and the penalty for walking away (para. 68). Assets dedicated to the contract are tested for impairment first (para. 69). Since the 2020 amendment the cost of fulfilling includes both incremental costs and an allocation of other directly related costs. For example (illustration), a subsidiary has a drilling services contract costing 60,000 for 2025, and the programme is cancelled. Exiting costs a penalty of 25,000; re-selling the services to another operator would bring in 40,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost of fulfilling, net of benefits = 60,000 − 40,000 = 20,000; penalty = 25,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Provision = lower of the two = 20,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Loss on onerous contract 20,000 / Cr Provision for onerous contract 20,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Restructuring.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A constructive obligation needs a detailed formal plan, started or announced to those affected (para. 72). Only direct costs not associated with continuing activities are included, so retraining staff who stay, marketing and new systems are excluded (paras. 80–81).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Decommissioning: the asset retirement obligation","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Installing a well, platform or pipeline creates an obligation to plug, remove and restore the site at the end of field life. PHE recognises it for \"oil and gas production facilities, wells, pipelines and related assets in accordance with the provisions in the PSC or in line with applicable regulations\" (Note 3o, Daftar 5/60, PDF p. 77). The Oil and gas properties essay shows the asset side; this section follows the liability.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Initial recognition.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The provision is recognised on installation and grows as more wells are drilled. The cost goes into the asset under PSAK 216.16(c) (formerly PSAK 16, IAS 16), because removing the platform is part of what it costs to use it. The removal cost has no future benefit of its own, so it is not a separate asset (following Kieso). Where the obligation arises from producing inventory during a period, as when a mine disturbs more land to extract this year's ore, the cost goes to inventory under PSAK 202 instead.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary completes an offshore platform with 20 wells on 31 December 2024. Removal and plugging are expected at the end of 2036, 12 years later. In today's prices the engineers see a 60 percent chance of 80,000, 30 percent of 100,000 and 10 percent of 140,000. Cost inflation is 2.5 percent and the pre-tax nominal discount rate 5.0 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expected cost in today's prices = 60% × 80,000 + 30% × 100,000 + 10% × 140,000 = 92,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In 2036 prices = 92,000 × 1.025^12 = 123,730","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Present value at 31 December 2024 = 123,730 / 1.05^12 = 68,897","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"One platform is a single obligation, so 80,000 is the most likely outcome. The other outcomes are all higher, so the best estimate lies above it, and this page uses the weighted 92,000, as section 3 allowed.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Oil and gas properties 68,897 / Cr Provision for decommissioning 68,897","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Unwinding of the discount.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" As settlement approaches, the present value rises by the discount rate. The increase is a ","type":"text"},{"text":"finance cost","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (para. 60) that ISAK 101 (formerly ISAK 9, IFRIC 1) sends to profit or loss and forbids capitalising. PHE calls it accretion.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025: 68,897 × 5% = 3,445; provision = 72,342","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2026: 72,342 × 5% = 3,617; provision = 75,959","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Finance cost 3,445 / Cr Provision for decommissioning 3,445 (2026: 3,617)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Changes in estimate.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The expected cash flows, their timing and the discount rate all move the provision. Under ISAK 101, for an asset carried at cost, the change is added to or deducted from the asset and depleted over its remaining life. A deduction may not exceed the asset's carrying amount, and any excess goes to profit. An addition is considered as a possible impairment indicator and, if it is one, the asset is tested. Once the asset reaches the end of its useful life, every later change goes to profit or loss. PHE's policy states the add/deduct rule, the cap and the impairment check (Note 3o).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Suppose that at the end of 2026 the market rate for this liability falls to 4.0 percent, with 10 years to go and the cash estimate unchanged.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Remeasured provision = 123,730 / 1.04^10 = 83,588; increase = 83,588 − 75,959 = 7,629","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Oil and gas properties 7,629 / Cr Provision for decommissioning 7,629","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"From 2027 the provision unwinds at 4.0 percent. Now suppose that at the end of 2033, with three years left, a cheaper plugging method cuts the 2036 estimate by 12,000, and the platform's carrying amount is down to 6,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Provision before the change = 123,730 / 1.04^3 = 109,996","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Decrease = 12,000 / 1.04^3 = 10,668; new provision = 99,328","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Provision for decommissioning 10,668 / Cr Oil and gas properties 6,000 / Cr Gain on decommissioning estimate 4,668","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Settlement.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The provision is used only for its original purpose (para. 61). By the end of 2036 the provision has unwound to the revised nominal estimate of 123,730 − 12,000 = 111,730. If the contractor charges 115,000, the 3,270 difference is a loss of that period.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Provision for decommissioning 111,730, Dr Loss on settlement 3,270 / Cr Cash 115,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Sensitivity.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A long-dated provision moves sharply with its discount rate. Take PHE's 2024 balance as the starting point, and treat it, for illustration only, as one payment 15 years away at 3.17 percent, the ratio of PHE's 2024 accretion to its opening balance (Note 29).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Implied payment in 15 years = 3,826,222 × 1.0317^15 = 6,110,428","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At 4.17%: 6,110,428 / 1.0417^15 = 3,310,790, a fall of 515,432 (13.5%)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At 2.17%: 6,110,428 / 1.0217^15 = 4,428,134, a rise of 601,912 (15.7%)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The rise exceeds the fall because present value is convex in the rate. Under ISAK 101 most of either swing would land on oil and gas properties, not profit.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Contingent liabilities and contingent assets","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A ","type":"text"},{"text":"contingent liability","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is either a possible obligation whose existence will be confirmed only by uncertain future events outside the entity's control, or a present obligation that fails the probability or estimate test (para. 10). It is never recognised (para. 27). It is disclosed, with an estimate of its effect where practicable, unless remote (paras. 28 and 86), and becomes a provision in the period the outflow turns probable (para. 30).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Joint and several liability.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The part of a joint obligation expected to be met by the others is a contingent liability, and the entity provides only for its own probable share (para. 29). For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary holds 30 percent of a block whose partners are jointly and severally liable for a 10,000 remediation order.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Provision = 30% × 10,000 = 3,000; contingent liability disclosed = 7,000, unless the partners look unable to pay","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE discloses a real case. A 2022 ICC award orders Pertamina E&P Libya and Pertamina, jointly and severally, to pay Libya's national oil company 24,000 and 5,200 plus interest for unfulfilled exploration commitments (Note 45e(i), Daftar 5/197–5/198, PDF pp. 214–215).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE is also guarantor for a former Australian subsidiary's share of abandonment costs in the Basker Manta Gummy block. The operator sued in Victoria; PHE's position, on legal advice, is that it owes nothing because abandonment was not completed within five years of withdrawal (Note 45e(ii), Daftar 5/198–5/202, PDF pp. 215–219). The note discloses no amount and does not say whether a provision is held. Its place under Note 45, \"commitments and contingencies\", with no matching provision in Notes 28 or 29, is consistent with a contingent liability.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A ","type":"text"},{"text":"contingent asset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is a possible asset from past events, such as a claim being pursued in court (paras. 10 and 32). It is not recognised (paras. 31 and 33), is disclosed when an inflow is probable (para. 34), and is recognised only when virtually certain (para. 35). A liability is booked at more likely than not, an asset only at virtually certain. For example (illustration), an insurance claim of 5,000 disputed at year end is disclosed; when the insurer accepts it in writing next year, it is booked.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Insurance claim receivable 5,000 / Cr Other income 5,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Decommissioning funds and the other non-current liabilities","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Funds set aside.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Under IFRIC 5 a contributor to a decommissioning fund keeps its obligation as a liability and recognises its interest in the fund separately; without control or influence over the fund, the reimbursement right is measured at the lower of the provision and its share of the fair value of the fund's net assets. IAI's renumbering list has no ISAK for IFRIC 5, but PSAK 237.53 and the offsetting ban in PSAK 201 (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1) give the same gross answer. For example (illustration), a provision of 10,000 and a fund of 6,000 appear as a liability of 10,000 and an asset of 6,000, never a net liability of 4,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's funds sit in joint accounts with SKK Migas, usable only for restoration with its approval, and are presented as restricted cash (Note 3o, Daftar 5/61, PDF p. 78), 1,890,493 non-current at the end of 2024 (Note 7, Daftar 5/93–5/95, PDF pp. 110–112), plus its share of joint-operation funds, 66,719, in other non-current assets (Note 14b, Daftar 5/105, PDF p. 122). Their fall from 296,555 is mostly a transfer into PHR's joint account (p. 123).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other non-current liabilities.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PHE's Note 28 shows why a liability with \"contingent\" in its name need not be a PSAK 237 item (Daftar 5/144, PDF p. 161).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2023","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Governing standard","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Acquisition of the Masela block","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"167,902","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"159,311","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 103, contingent consideration","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Penalty for unfulfilled work commitments (Libya)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"39,600","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"39,600","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"liability; possibly the ICC award in Note 45e(i), not stated","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Monitoring and recovery of two wells","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"25,948","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"25,959","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 237, decommissioning-type","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Utilisation of the Badak LNG plant","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"18,313","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"22,503","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"obligation under PHI's WBX-NR sales contract; standard not stated","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSC partner","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10,387","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9,100","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"not explained","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Others","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"19,078","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"17,312","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"includes Simenggaris sunk cost 9,720 and abandonment costs 7,530","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"281,228","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"273,774","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE Masela bought a 20 percent participating interest from Shell, completed on 18 October 2023 (Note 5f, Daftar 5/88, PDF p. 105), and owes a further payment if the block's partners take a final investment decision (FID). Under PSAK 103 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Business Combinations","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 22, IFRS 3) contingent consideration is part of the price, recognised at fair value at the acquisition date (PHE measures it at present value under PSAK 113, Note 28) and, if it is a liability, remeasured at fair value through profit or loss (Note 3d, Daftar 5/36, PDF p. 53). PHE applies PSAK 103 to this acquired interest in a joint operation, consistent with PSAK 111's rule for interests that are businesses. PSAK 237 does not apply, both because another standard covers it (para. 5) and because a contractual obligation to pay cash is a financial liability (para. 2).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The two standards treat the same odds differently. For example (illustration), an acquirer will pay 100,000 if FID is taken in four years, judged 40 percent likely, with a discount rate of 8 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under PSAK 237 logic: outflow 40% < 50%, nothing recognised, contingent liability disclosed","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under PSAK 103: fair value = 40% × 100,000 / 1.08^4 = 29,401, recognised as part of the cost of the acquisition","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A year later the odds rise to 60%: 60% × 100,000 / 1.08^3 = 47,630; Dr Loss on remeasurement 18,229 / Cr Contingent consideration liability 18,229","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Provisions across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Mining.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Indonesian miners provide for reclamation and post-mining closure under PP 78/2010 and also place guarantees, such as time deposits, that do not discharge the obligation, so both are shown gross. Power plants carry the same provision. Mining also tests section 5's asset-or-inventory split every year. For example (illustration), a coal pit incurs a restoration obligation of 2,000 for land disturbed to extract this year's coal and 3,000 for a haul road built to serve the mine for ten years.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Inventories, coal 2,000 / Cr Provision for reclamation 2,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Mine properties 3,000 / Cr Provision for reclamation 3,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lessees and telecom towers.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Restoring leased land puts the cost into the right-of-use asset and the obligation into a PSAK 237 provision (see the Right-of-use essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Levies.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" ISAK 121 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Levies","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly ISAK 30, IFRIC 21) makes the obligating event the activity that triggers payment under the law, not the period the levy relates to. For example (illustration), a levy of 1,200 for a year that is triggered by being in operation on 1 January is a full liability of 1,200 on 1 January, not 100 a month.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Banks and insurers.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Financial guarantees and loan commitments fall under PSAK 109 (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9), with expected credit losses, not under PSAK 237 (para. 2). Insurers' claims liabilities fall under PSAK 117 (formerly PSAK 74, IFRS 17).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 29","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 237 asks for a reconciliation showing additions, amounts used, unused amounts reversed and the unwinding (para. 84), and for the expected timing, uncertainties, major assumptions and reimbursements (para. 85). PHE gives a three-line movement (Note 29, Daftar 5/145, PDF p. 162).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Balance at beginning of year","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,784,917","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,460,330","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Additions/(deductions) and revisions of estimates","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"78,620","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"211,366","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accretion expense","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"119,925","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"113,221","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Balance at end of year","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,826,222","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,784,917","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2023: 3,460,330 + 211,366 + 113,221 = 3,784,917","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024: 3,784,917 + 78,620 + 119,925 = 3,983,462, not 3,826,222; 3,784,917 − 78,620 + 119,925 = 3,826,222","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 2024 revisions only foot as a deduction of 78,620, though they are printed without brackets. Alternatively PHE omitted a line: if 78,620 is an addition, an unprinted reduction of 157,240 is needed. The Oil and gas properties essay gives the same two readings. Three more points stand out.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"There is no line for amounts used. Either no abandonment spending was charged in two years, or it is netted inside the revisions, though paragraph 84 asks for it separately.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 51 reports non-cash decommissioning adjustments to oil and gas properties of +12,627 in 2024 and 202,685 in 2023 (Daftar 5/235, PDF p. 252), against Note 29 revisions of 78,620 (a deduction on the footing reading) and 211,366. The gaps of 91,247 and 8,681 could be utilisation, revisions taken to profit, disposals or exchange differences; the FS do not say.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The discount rate, inflation assumption and timing are not disclosed. Note 29 describes ARO as the present value of costs \"expected to be incurred during the terms of the PSCs\" and Note 4b(ii) lists the main uncertainties (Daftar 5/80, PDF p. 97).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accretion / opening provision = 119,925 / 3,784,917 = 3.17% (2023: 113,221 / 3,460,330 = 3.27%)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accretion / finance cost = 119,925 / 453,906 = 26.4% (Note 41, Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Funds held / provision = (1,890,493 + 66,719) / 3,826,222 = 51.2% (2023: 52.8%)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deferred tax on the provision = 66,352 + 412,586 = 478,938, or 12.5% of the provision (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"About half the obligation, at present value, is backed by cash usable for nothing else. The deferred tax ratio is far below PHE's tax rates, plausibly because ASR deposits are recoverable or deductible when placed (section 10), leaving much of the provision with no future deduction; the mechanics belong to the income tax essays. Note 28's 2023 lines also add to 273,785, not the 273,774 printed, a difference of 11.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Upstream note: abandonment and site restoration under the PSC","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Indonesian upstream contractors set aside abandonment and site restoration (ASR) funds under Minister of Energy and Mineral Resources Regulation 15/2018, starting in the year production becomes commercial, in a joint account with SKK Migas at a state-owned bank. Under cost recovery the amounts set aside are recoverable costs; under gross split they are deductible for income tax. PHE OSES, for example, deposited funds for both halves of 2024 on SKK Migas instruction (Note 45i, Daftar 5/208, PDF p. 225).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"oil-gas-properties-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the asset that absorbs the initial estimate and later revisions.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"restricted-cash-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/restricted-cash","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the ASR funds.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-non-current-assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": funds in joint operations.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"finance-cost","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/finance-cost","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the accretion.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred-tax-liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the deductible temporary difference on the provision.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade-payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-payables","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"accrued-expenses","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/accrued-expenses","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the liabilities with certain or nearly certain amounts.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-non-current-liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":": covered here, section 7.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That PHE treats the Basker Manta Gummy claim as a contingent liability is inferred from its place in Note 45 (commitments and contingencies) and the absence of an amount or provision. The item itself is not labelled.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Reading Note 29's 78,620 as a deduction rests on footing alone; PHE may instead have omitted a line of 157,240. The reasons for the gaps to Note 51 are not disclosed, and the listed causes are possibilities, not findings. That ASR deposits explain the low deferred tax ratio is this page's reading.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 3.17 percent \"rate\" and the single 15-year payment in the sensitivity are illustrative simplifications. PHE discloses neither its discount rate nor the timing, and a portfolio of fields with different rates and dates would respond differently.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That Indonesia has no ISAK equivalent of IFRIC 5 rests on IAI's renumbering list, which lists only interpretations in force. Whether PHE's joint ASR accounts would fall within IFRIC 5's scope if it applied is this page's reading; PHE presents them as restricted cash.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The governing standards given for the Note 28 items are this page's reading of one-line descriptions. The Libya penalty may be the ICC award of Note 45e(i); the FS do not link them, and the balance shows no interest accrual. The 51.2 percent funding ratio assumes all non-current restricted cash is ASR funds, as Note 7's description implies but does not state line by line.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); Note 3d (Daftar 5/36, PDF p. 53); Note 3o (Daftar 5/60–5/61, PDF pp. 77–78); Note 4b(ii) (Daftar 5/80, PDF p. 97); Note 5f (Daftar 5/88, PDF p. 105); Note 7 (Daftar 5/93–5/95, PDF pp. 110–112); Note 14b (Daftar 5/105–5/106, PDF pp. 122–123); Note 28 (Daftar 5/144, PDF p. 161); Note 29 (Daftar 5/145, PDF p. 162); Note 41 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172); Note 45e(i)–(ii) (Daftar 5/197–5/202, PDF pp. 214–219); Note 45i (Daftar 5/208, PDF p. 225); Note 51 (Daftar 5/235, PDF p. 252). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk; text dump, Indonesian and English columns read).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 237 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Provisi, Liabilitas Kontinjensi, dan Aset Kontinjensi","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 37), paras. 1–2, 5, 7, 10–35, 36–43, 45–55, 59–61, 63, 65–69, 72, 80–81, 84–86, 89; para. 5(f) on contingent consideration re-checked in the current consolidated text (faronline.se). VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation text of IAS 37 reproduced at ktkt.uel.edu.vn; that consolidation predates the 2020 amendment). The 2020 \"Onerous Contracts — Cost of Fulfilling a Contract\" amendment: VERIFIED in the Inventories essay (web, IFRS Foundation).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"ISAK 101 (IFRIC 1 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Changes in Existing Decommissioning, Restoration and Similar Liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"): cost model, cap at carrying amount, impairment indicator, end of useful life, unwinding as finance cost and no capitalisation. VERIFIED (web, BDO New Zealand summary of NZ IFRIC 1, 2025). Paragraph numbers not cited.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IFRIC 5 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Rights to Interests arising from Decommissioning, Restoration and Environmental Rehabilitation Funds","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": separate recognition and the lower-of measurement. VERIFIED (web, IAS Plus; BDO NZ summary). No ISAK 105 or other ISAK for IFRIC 5 in the list: VERIFIED (web, IAI, \"Komparasi Perubahan Penomoran PSAK ISAK SAK Indonesia\"; confirmed in the independent recheck). IFRIC 5.9 lower-of measurement: VERIFIED (web, secondary, BDO).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK numbers PSAK 237 (57), PSAK 103 (22), PSAK 111 (66), PSAK 113 (68), PSAK 117 (74), ISAK 101 (ISAK 9), ISAK 121 (ISAK 30): VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list). PSAK 103 contingent consideration (paras. 39–40, 58), PSAK 111.21A (acquired interests in joint operations that are businesses), PSAK 216.16(c) and 18, PSAK 202, PSAK 109, PSAK 117, ISAK 121 content: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED here, except as quoted in PHE's Note 3d.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", IFRS edition, chapter on current liabilities, provisions and contingencies. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Peraturan Menteri ESDM No. 15/2018 on post-operation activities in upstream oil and gas: ASR funds, joint account with SKK Migas, cost recovery and gross split treatment. VERIFIED (primary: Berita Negara 2018 No. 305, Arts. 11(2)(b), 11(3), 12(1)–(2), checked in the independent recheck; also SSEK, Ditjen Migas).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PP No. 78/2010 on reclamation and post-mining, with Permen ESDM 26/2018: reclamation and post-mining guarantees. VERIFIED (web, secondary: BPK regional notes, law-firm summaries). Current amendments not checked.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of provisions and contingencies from recognition to disclosure, with asset retirement obligations as the main application, worked through PHE's largest liability and set beside the industries with their own rules","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["A provision is a liability of uncertain timing or amount. It is recognised when a past event has left a present obligation the entity cannot realistically avoid, an outflow is more likely than not, and the amount can be estimated. Anything weaker is a contingent liability, disclosed unless remote.","A provision is measured at the best estimate of what it would rationally cost to settle the obligation today. That is an expected value over a large population or the most likely outcome adjusted for skew for a single obligation, discounted at a pre-tax rate that matches the cash flows (nominal with nominal, real with real).","A decommissioning obligation is a provision whose cost is added to the asset that caused it. The discount unwinds as a finance cost, and changes in the estimate or the rate move the asset, not profit, until the asset is used up. PHE carries 3,826,222 of it, its largest liability."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = '8e26845c-467c-4c83-b593-a0c647e354be' AND updated_at = '2026-10-12T09:47:08.318531+00:00'::timestamptz;
INSERT INTO public.essay_revisions (essay_id, revision_no, change_type, title, snippet, content_json, status, change_summary, changed_by)
SELECT e.id, coalesce((SELECT max(x.revision_no) FROM public.essay_revisions x WHERE x.essay_id = e.id), 0) + 1, 'migration',
       e.title, e.snippet, e.content_json, e.status, $s$Lecture-notes source removed and references renumbered (site cleanup 2026-10, WP3)$s$, null
  FROM public.essays e WHERE e.id = '8e26845c-467c-4c83-b593-a0c647e354be';

DO $chk$ BEGIN
  IF (SELECT count(*) FROM public.essays WHERE id IN ('31865371-572a-48b7-96fe-03d502d3ddf9', '4146bf74-cfa6-4833-a95c-11f44131c80f', 'd7acd544-a882-41bc-b9de-68981b29ade8', '8e26845c-467c-4c83-b593-a0c647e354be') AND updated_at = now()) <> 4 THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Not written: an essay in this part was changed after the snapshot. Nothing in this part was saved.';
  END IF;
END $chk$;
